      <p class="text-sm text-gray-500">Plans scale up to KVM 64GB at $250/mo unmanaged / $295/mo managed. Every tier includes 10 Gbps DDoS protection, full root access, and unmetered bandwidth with the fair-use caps above. The cPanel/WHM managed add-on is $35.99/mo. Source: iozoom.com/linux-vps (August 2026).</p>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mt-10 mb-4">Virtualization & Operating System</h2>
      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-white mt-6 mb-3">True KVM, No Artificial Restrictions</h3>
      <p>Every IO Zoom plan runs <strong>KVM</strong> (Kernel-based Virtual Machine), a type-1 hypervisor built into Linux. The practical difference vs shared hosting or container-based VPS is real: each instance gets <strong>full root access</strong> and IO Zoom does not restrict what you can install or run. For developers who want control over the kernel, custom software stacks, or resource-hungry workloads, that's a meaningful difference — and unusual to see stated this plainly on a $10/mo plan.</p>

      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-white mt-6 mb-3">Open-Source Linux Only</h3>
      <p>IO Zoom offers <strong>four open-source distributions</strong>: CentOS, Fedora, Ubuntu, and Debian. All are freely available and well documented. There's no Windows VPS tier advertised on the Linux VPS page, so Windows workloads are not a fit here — but for Linux-first developers, the open-source-only lineup keeps everything free and auditable.</p>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mt-10 mb-4">Data Center Footprint (8 Locations)</h2>
      <p>IO Zoom's <strong>8-datacenter footprint</strong> is its strongest differentiator among budget KVM hosts, which often ship with only 1–3 US locations:</p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-gray-300">
        <li><strong>US East:</strong> Atlanta (GA), New Jersey, Miami (FL)</li>
        <li><strong>US West/Central:</strong> Los Angeles (CA), Chicago (IL), Dallas (TX)</li>
        <li><strong>Europe:</strong> Frankfurt (Germany)</li>
      </ul>
      <p>This lets latency-sensitive workloads pick a location near their end users — a real advantage for a provider starting at $10/mo. It's not the 30+ region footprint of the big clouds (Vultr runs 32+, DigitalOcean dozens), but for most small deployments, 8 well-placed DCs across the US and EU is plenty, and it beats most budget hosts comfortably.</p>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mt-10 mb-4">Network & Performance</h2>
      <p>Io Zoom advertises <strong>10 Gbps DDoS protection on every plan</strong> — unusual at the budget tier, where DDoS filtering is often an add-on or gated to higher plans. Combined with <strong>unmetered bandwidth</strong> (subject to fair-use caps that scale 2 TB → 12 TB with plan size), the network story is a genuine strength. There's no published bandwidth overage fee structure, which is friendlier than metered hosts that bill per GB.</p>
      <p>The trade-off: IO Zoom does not publish an <strong>explicit uptime SLA</strong> with a credit policy. For mission-critical production workloads where a documented 99.9%+ guarantee matters, that's a gap you should weigh against budget hosts that do publish SLA terms.</p>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mt-10 mb-4">Who Should Choose IO Zoom?</h2>
      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-white mt-6 mb-3">1. Budget Developers Who Want Full Root Access</h3>
      <p>At <strong>$10/mo for a 2 vCPU / 2 GB KVM</strong> with full root and 10 Gbps DDoS standard, IO Zoom is a strong fit for developers who want a real virtual server without paying for management they don't need. The free migrations also lower the switching cost if you're coming from another host.</p>

      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-white mt-6 mb-3">2. Latency-Sensitive Workloads Needing a US/EU Footprint</h3>
      <p>The <strong>8 locations across the US and Europe</strong> — including both coasts plus Frankfurt — make IO Zoom competitive for serving multiple regional audiences from a single provider at a low price. That's the strongest case for choosing it over a 1–2 DC budget host.</p>

      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-white mt-6 mb-3">3. Linux-First Users Who Prefer Open Source</h3>
      <p>CentOS, Fedora, Ubuntu, and Debian cover the mainstream open-source stack. If you run your whole toolchain on free software and want nothing proprietary in the base image, IO Zoom's open-source-only lineup aligns well.</p>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mt-10 mb-4">Where IO Zoom Falls Short</h2>
      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-white mt-6 mb-3">No Explicit Uptime SLA</h3>
      <p>IO Zoom does not publish a documented uptime guarantee or credit policy. Budget hosts without an SLA aren't rare, but if your workload needs a contractual availability commitment, you'll want a provider that publishes one — <a href="/vps/racknerd" class="text-orange-400 hover:underline">RackNerd</a> publishes a 99.9% uptime guarantee on its terms, and managed hosts like <a href="/vps/tmdhosting" class="text-orange-400 hover:underline">TMDHosting</a> advertise 99.99%.</p>

      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-white mt-6 mb-3">Entry Price Is Up From Historical Positioning</h3>
      <p>IO Zoom's <strong>$10/mo unmanaged entry</strong> is well above the ~$3–4/mo their plans historically started at (and above RackNerd's $1.99 and Vultr's $5 entries). It reflects broader cost inflation, but it also means IO Zoom is no longer the cheapest option on the board — it competes on <em>value per feature</em> (root + DDoS + 8 DCs), not on absolute lowest price.</p>

      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-white mt-6 mb-3">cPanel-Only Management, No Windows</h3>
      <p>If you want a control panel, it's <strong>cPanel only</strong> ($35.99/mo license on top of the $20/mo managed plan). There's no Plesk option advertised, and no Windows VPS tier on the Linux page. Windows workloads or Plesk shops should look elsewhere — for example <a href="/vps/hostarmada" class="text-orange-400 hover:underline">HostArmada</a> or <a href="/vps/interserver" class="text-orange-400 hover:underline">InterServer</a>.</p>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mt-10 mb-4">Comparison: IO Zoom vs Other VPS Providers on VPSTier</h2>
      <p>We've reviewed 60+ VPS providers on vpstier.com. Here's how IO Zoom stacks up to the closest comparables.</p>

      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-white mt-6 mb-3">IO Zoom vs RackNerd</h3>
      <p><a href="/vps/racknerd" class="text-orange-400 hover:underline">RackNerd</a> starts at $1.99/mo for 1 vCPU / 1 GB RAM / 25 GB SSD with a broad US datacenter footprint and a 99.9% uptime guarantee. IO Zoom's $10/mo entry is 5× that, but it bundles 10 Gbps DDoS protection standard plus a European (Frankfurt) location and more RAM at the entry tier. RackNerd remains the absolute-budget leader; IO Zoom wins when you want the DDoS coverage and a EU/US mix without leaving the budget tier.</p>

      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-white mt-6 mb-3">IO Zoom vs Vultr</h3>
      <p><a href="/vps/vultr" class="text-orange-400 hover:underline">Vultr</a> starts at $5/mo for 1 vCPU / 1 GB / 25 GB with true hourly billing and 32+ locations — but everything is fully self-managed and DDoS protection is not bundled at the entry tier the way IO Zoom does. IO Zoom's 8 DCs are far fewer, but the $10 entry gives you 2 GB RAM plus 10 Gbps DDoS standard. Vultr wins on scale and hourly flexibility; IO Zoom on bundled network protection per dollar.</p>

      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-white mt-6 mb-3">IO Zoom vs DigitalOcean</h3>
      <p><a href="/vps/digitalocean" class="text-orange-400 hover:underline">DigitalOcean</a> starts at $6/mo for 1 vCPU / 1 GB with a polished control panel, managed database/add-on ecosystem, and many global regions — but everything is separate-priced (DDoS protection is an add-on, not standard). IO Zoom's $10 entry bundles DDoS and root access with a smaller but genuinely global footprint. DigitalOcean is the better platform play; IO Zoom the leaner, lower-configuration budget server.</p>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mt-10 mb-4">FAQ: IO Zoom VPS in 2026</h2>

      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-white mt-6 mb-3">What is the cheapest IO Zoom VPS plan?</h3>
      <p>The cheapest IO Zoom unmanaged Linux VPS is <strong>KVM 2GB at $10/month</strong> — 2 vCPU, 2 GB RAM, 20 GB SSD, 2 TB bandwidth, 10 Gbps DDoS protection, and full root access. Managed with cPanel is $20/month. Plans scale to KVM 64GB at $250/mo unmanaged / $295/mo managed.</p>

      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-white mt-6 mb-3">Is IO Zoom VPS managed or unmanaged?</h3>
      <p>IO Zoom offers both. Entry plans are <strong>unmanaged</strong> (self-managed Linux with full root) at $10/month, or <strong>managed with cPanel</strong> at $20/month. The cPanel/WHM license add-on is $35.99/month; IO Zoom lists cPanel as its control panel and does not advertise Plesk.</p>

      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-white mt-6 mb-3">Where are IO Zoom's data centers?</h3>
      <p>IO Zoom operates <strong>8 data centers</strong> across the US and Europe: Atlanta (GA), New Jersey, Miami (FL), Los Angeles (CA), Chicago (IL), and Dallas (TX) in the US, plus Frankfurt (Germany) in Europe.</p>

      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-white mt-6 mb-3">Does IO Zoom include DDoS protection?</h3>
      <p>Yes — <strong>every IO Zoom KVM plan includes 10 Gbps DDoS protection as standard</strong>, uncommon at the $10/mo budget tier. Plans also ship with unmetered bandwidth subject to fair-use caps from 2 TB (KVM 2GB) up to 12 TB on larger tiers.</p>

      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-white mt-6 mb-3">How does IO Zoom compare to RackNerd and Vultr?</h3>
      <p>IO Zoom ($10/mo unmanaged) offers true KVM with full root and 10 Gbps DDoS standard across 8 US/EU DCs. RackNerd undercuts at $1.99/mo for 1 vCPU / 1 GB with a broad US footprint but without root-level DDoS at every tier. Vultr's $5/mo hourly cloud covers 32+ locations but is fully self-managed. IO Zoom sits between: more locations than RackNerd's budget line and cheaper than Vultr's smaller plans, but fewer regions than either's full catalog.</p>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mt-10 mb-4">Final Verdict</h2>
      <p>IO Zoom is a solid pick when <strong>full root access, 10 Gbps DDoS protection, and a genuinely global US/EU footprint matter more than absolute lowest price</strong>. The $10/mo entry with 2 GB RAM and DDoS standard beats most budget hosts on value-per-feature, and the 8-datacenter spread (including Frankfurt) is a real advantage for latency-sensitive workloads. It is not the cheapest (RackNerd at $1.99 undercuts by 5×), not the largest footprint (Vultr/DigitalOcean offer 30+ regions), and not the most documented on uptime (no explicit SLA).</p>

      <p>For a developer who wants a real, unrestricted KVM server with bundled network protection and doesn't need management overhead, IO Zoom delivers. For pure-budget unmanaged Linux at the lowest possible price, see our <a href="/vps/racknerd" class="text-orange-400 hover:underline">RackNerd review</a>. For hourly-billed self-managed cloud at scale, see <a href="/vps/vultr" class="text-orange-400 hover:underline">Vultr</a>. For a polish-and-ecosystem platform, see <a href="/vps/digitalocean" class="text-orange-400 hover:underline">DigitalOcean</a>.</p>

